th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/monomial-on-rn.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/monomial-on-rn.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the defining form of a monomial on $\\mathbb{R}^n$?","options":["Any function $f\\colon \\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}$ that is continuous.","A function of the form $a x_1^{k_1}\\cdots x_n^{k_n}$ with $a\\in\\mathbb{R}$ and $k_j\\in\\mathbb{N}$.","A sum of several terms with different powers of the variables.","A product of variables whose exponents may be any real numbers."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"af8d3220cb864c5df68cb3e6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How is the total degree of $a x_1^{k_1}\\cdots x_n^{k_n}$ defined?","options":["It is $a+k_1+\\cdots+k_n$.","It is the largest value among $a,k_1,\\ldots,k_n$.","It is $k_1+\\cdots+k_n$.","It is the number of nonzero variables in the input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3550edb5c36042f73374da1f","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression is a monomial on $\\mathbb{R}^2$?","options":["$3x^2y^0$","$x^{-1}y$","$x+y$","$\\sin(x)y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f895595afd1ff8c76e4e99b9","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What happens to the exponents when two monomials on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are multiplied?","options":["They multiply componentwise.","They are replaced by their maximum values.","They add componentwise.","They must all become $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b5ae1320a5986d21377f3500","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x,y) = 2x^3y$ and $g(x,y) = -5xy^2$. What is $f(x,y)g(x,y)$?","options":["$-10x^4y^3$","$-10x^3y^2$","$7x^4y^3$","$-3x^4y^3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4bb9df731c7adde4704cf385","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"One monomial has total degree $3$ and another has total degree $4$. What is the total degree of their product?","options":["$7$","$12$","$1$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6de02c7b5e414b185aaf620d","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_85bde1a2_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the total degree of the constant monomial $f(x,y)=5$?","options":["$0$","$5$","$1$","It has no total degree"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5e73176266f6ed5879926d1c","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_85bde1a2_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the total degree of $-3x^2y^4$?","options":["$6$","$4$","$2$","$-3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"670ea41b6089dc96044169b0","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_85bde1a2_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=x^3y$, how does $f(2x,2y)$ compare with $f(x,y)$?","options":["$f(2x,2y)=16\\,f(x,y)$","$f(2x,2y)=8\\,f(x,y)$","$f(2x,2y)=4\\,f(x,y)$","$f(2x,2y)=2\\,f(x,y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4b6a67538c1b36fd173ed7ba","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_e979973d_006_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly relates monomials on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to homogeneous functions?","options":["Every nonzero monomial is homogeneous, of degree equal to its total degree $k_1+\\cdots+k_n$.","A monomial is homogeneous only when it is linear, that is, of total degree $1$.","A monomial is homogeneous of degree equal to its largest exponent $\\max_j k_j$.","A monomial is homogeneous only when its coefficient $a$ is positive."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"956ad4eccfb4f478263d2b7e","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_monomial_on_rn_85bde1a2_017_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"On $(0,\\infty)^2$ the function $g(x,y)=x^{1/2}y^{1/2}$ satisfies $g(cx,cy)=c\\,g(x,y)$ for all $c>0$. What does this show?","options":["$g$ is homogeneous of degree $1$ without being a monomial: its exponents are not nonnegative integers.","$g$ is a monomial of total degree $1$, because its two exponents add up to $\\tfrac{1}{2}+\\tfrac{1}{2}=1$ as the definition requires.","$g$ is a monomial of total degree $\\tfrac{1}{2}$, the common exponent of its two variables.","$g$ is a polynomial on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ of degree $1$, since every function homogeneous of degree $1$ is linear."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1f610bb66ca37847d39e80bb","time_limit":50}]}}